{"t":1791579216,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"ef597bd4-949e-4d4d-9ba2-94bfcc726398","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-09T18:45:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 091845\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n245 PM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\n\nAdded a mention of possible gale conditions over the lower \nChesapeake Bay on Sunday as a result of passing remnants of \nHurricane Isaias. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to bring rain, breezy \n  conditions Sunday into Sunday night.\n\n- 2) No hazardous weather expected next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to bring rain, \nbreezy conditions Sunday into Sunday night.\n\nThe pattern remains quiet through the first half of the weekend\nas surface high pressure strengthens over the northeastern U.S.\nHowever, this lengthy period of tranquil weather will come to an\nend as this ridge retreats northward ahead of Isaias. Until\nthen, a thick area of high cirrus should continue to track\npoleward. Daily highs will be in the low/mid 70s, with 60s\nacross mountain locations.\n\nThe main weather impact to the region over the next week is going to \nbe from the remnants of what is now Hurricane Isaias (Cat 3\nstrength). Model guidance is in generally good agreement that \nHurricane Isaias' surface low weakens and broadens as the system\nmoves into the TN Valley Saturday night into Sunday. The mid-\nlevel circulation amplifies and becomes a strong cutoff low over\nKY/TN that moves slowly east across the Mid- Atlantic Sunday \ninto Monday. Periods of rain are likely across the area at times\nSaturday evening through Sunday night. Rainfall amounts of 1-2 \ninches are expected across the area. Higher rain totals are \ncertainly possible, but will depend on a few other smaller scale\nfeatures.\n\nTropical moisture surges north into our area Saturday night into \nSunday, with the highest PWAT values at or above 2\" (most likely \neast of the Blue Ridge). While the main cutoff low associated with \nthe remnants of Isaias remain west of the Appalachians on Saturday, \nexpect light rain to move in from south to north in the evening. \nForcing for ascent increases Saturday night, and especially Sunday \nmorning into Sunday afternoon as mid-level heights fall with the \napproach of the cutoff low. There is going to be enhanced low-level \nconvergence somewhere over the region as the surface high to the \neast and cutoff low to the west interact. Easterly flow into the \nBlue Ridge could also produce enhanced rain amounts due to \norographic effects. Additionally, an inverted trough or wave of low \npressure tries to develop in lee of the Appalachians Sunday as it \ncrosses Virginia to the Delmarva.\n\nUncertainty remains regarding the exact evolution and timing of \nthese features, which will ultimately determine where / if any \nlocally heavy rain occurs. Continue to monitor the forecast over the \nnext couple of days. No Flood Watches are planned at this time as we \nare still over 48 hours from the main slug of rain.\nAdditionally, there is a non-zero risk of isolated tornadoes on\nSunday. These post-tropical environments can be tricky as low-\nlevel spin ups do occur. For now, the Storm Prediction Center\nhas maintained a Day 3 Marginal Risk area.\n\nIt is going to be noticeably breezy, especially in the mountains \nwhere winds begin to pick up Saturday evening. The strongest winds \nare most likely on Sunday. The current forecast for peak wind gusts \nis 30-40 mph in the Alleghenies and 20-30 mph elsewhere.\n\nConditions dry out Monday morning into Monday afternoon as the \nremnants of Isaias push offshore. Looking at mostly mild \ntemperatures for most of next week. A series of cold fronts cross \nthe region during the middle to late part of next week as the \nsynoptic pattern becomes more active. Aside from mountain rain \nshowers, most of the area looks to remain dry. The strongest cold \nfront arrives late next week, possibly bringing rain and colder air \nbehind it.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...No hazardous weather expected next week. \n\nIn the wake of the remnants of Isaias, high pressure will build \nbriefly over the area Monday night and Tuesday. A mainly dry cold \nfront will move across the area late Tuesday night, but may bring \nsome scattered-numerous rain showers to the Allegheny Mountains late \nTuesday night into Wednesday in upslope flow. \n\nTemperatures will start trending cooler during the middle and second \nhalf of next toward near normal. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\n\nVFR conditions expected throughout the day Saturday. Beginning \nSaturday night and into Sunday, lowering CIGs and VSBYs are \nanticipated as the remnants of Hurricane Isaias move in from the \nwest. Low clouds and reduced visibility from steady rain will be the \nprimary impacts. While not directly impacting forecasted airfield \nsites, some low-level wind shear is possible across the Blue Ridge \non Sunday morning as the system approaches for any low-flying \naircraft. Wind gusts up to 20-30 knots possible throughout the day \nSunday as the system moves through.\n\nAs the system begins to transition east offshore late Sunday into \nMonday, winds should diminish. VFR conditions return Monday and \nTuesday as weak high pressure builds in.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nWinds stay below SCA criteria through Saturday afternoon, then \ngradually pick up overnight. Remnants from Hurricane Isaias will \ntrack through the Mid-Atlantic throughout the day Sunday, with winds \nup to 30 knots across the Chesapeake Bay possible. Winds will be \nstronger to the south, and could approach gale thresholds before \ndiminishing some overnight as the system passes through. Winds \nlikely stay within SCA criteria through Monday, diminishing by\nTuesday.\n\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\n\nMinor coastal flooding seems likely at sensitive locations Sunday \nevening due to rising water levels due to strengthening east to \nsoutheast winds combined with high astronomical tides due to a new \nmoon. Based on the latest forecast, Annapolis could near\nmoderate flooding levels on Sunday evening, but confidence is\nlow at this time. Winds turn offshore Monday allowing tide \nlevels to recede.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory from 4 AM to 7 PM EDT Sunday for MDZ008.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory from 4 AM to 7 PM EDT Sunday for ANZ530-\n     531-535-538-539.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 1 AM to 7 PM EDT Sunday for ANZ532-\n     533-536-540>542.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 7 PM Saturday to 7 PM EDT Sunday for \n     ANZ534-537-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/BRO/KRR/SRT\nAVIATION...LFR/KRR/SRT\nMARINE...LFR/KRR/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791579175,"days":{"2026-10-09":{"hi":76,"lo":54,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-10-10":{"hi":72,"lo":61,"pop":65,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-10-11":{"hi":69,"lo":64,"pop":99,"short":"Rain then Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-10-12":{"hi":74,"lo":60,"pop":28,"short":"Chance Rain Showers then Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-13":{"hi":79,"lo":59,"pop":13,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-14":{"hi":70,"lo":53,"pop":18,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-15":{"hi":65,"lo":54,"pop":20,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1791576000,"n":48,"ceil":[25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,null,null,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,24900,24300,23800,23200,22500,21900,21200,20500,19800,19100,14900,10800,6600,6000,5500,4900,4000,3000,2100,1800,1600,1300,9000,9000,9000,9000,9000,9000,9000],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10